  <Document>
    <name>🌞 LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</h2><p><b>Data time:</b> 2026-09-26 05:00 AM EDT<br/><b>Source updated:</b> n/a (NOMADS)<br/><b>Source version:</b> rap-20260926-t09z-f01<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-26 06:23 AM EDT<br/><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/solar_radiation/legend.png?v=rap-20260926-t09z-f01" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Surface downward shortwave solar radiation (W/m^2), RAP hourly estimate, FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;1000+: night <b>0</b> (near-black) &rarr; <b>150</b> &rarr; <b>300</b> &rarr; <b>450</b> &rarr; <b>600</b> &rarr; <b>750</b> &rarr; extreme <b>1000+</b> (near-white). <b>LOWEST 0</b>, <b>HIGHEST+ 1000+</b>. Same flux always shows the same color. Values shown exactly as observed; night reads zero; clouds reduce values quantitatively. Broadband sunlight: includes ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared energy. This is not a UV Index and not a visible-light meter reading. Hourly weather-model estimate, not a ground sensor measurement at every location.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP RAP (Rapid Refresh) hourly surface DSWRF (downward short-wave radiation flux)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/rap/prod/">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/rap/prod/</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</h2><p>Modeled incoming sunlight energy at the surface (broadband: includes ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared solar energy). This is not a UV Index and not a visible-light meter reading. An hourly weather-model estimate, not a ground sensor measurement at every location. Nighttime reads zero (valid data, near-black) — not missing data. Clouds reduce values quantitatively versus clear sky.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/solar_radiation/legend.png?v=rap-20260926-t09z-f01" width="600" alt="key"></p><p>Surface downward shortwave solar radiation (W/m^2), RAP hourly estimate, FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;1000+: night <b>0</b> (near-black) &rarr; <b>150</b> &rarr; <b>300</b> &rarr; <b>450</b> &rarr; <b>600</b> &rarr; <b>750</b> &rarr; extreme <b>1000+</b> (near-white). <b>LOWEST 0</b>, <b>HIGHEST+ 1000+</b>. Same flux always shows the same color. Values shown exactly as observed; night reads zero; clouds reduce values quantitatively. Broadband sunlight: includes ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared energy. This is not a UV Index and not a visible-light meter reading. Hourly weather-model estimate, not a ground sensor measurement at every location.</p><p><b>Variable:</b> DSWRF surface, newest RAP cycle valid for the current hour (analysis step when present, else shortest forecast lead)<br/><b>Units:</b> W/m^2 (hourly RAP cycle; value valid for the current hour (lead time recorded per run))<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP RAP (Rapid Refresh) hourly surface DSWRF (downward short-wave radiation flux)<br/><b>Update:</b> RAP hourly cycles; value valid for the current hour<br/><b>Data time:</b> 2026-09-26 05:00 AM EDT<br/><b>Source version:</b> rap-20260926-t09z-f01<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-26 06:23 AM EDT<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/rap/prod/">NOMADS RAP</a></p>]]></description>
